  "content": {
    "id": "p32-q6-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "6",
    "prompt": "",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p32-q6a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Find $a$ and $b$ for which\n$$\\frac{1}{(n-1)(n+1)}=\\frac{a}{n-1}+\\frac{b}{n+1}.$$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$a=\\frac12$, $b=-\\frac12$.",
          "worked": "Clearing denominators gives\n$$\\begin{align*}1&=a(n+1)+b(n-1)\\\\1&=(a+b)n+a-b.\\end{align*}$$\nSetting $n=1$ and $n=-1$ in this polynomial identity gives\n$$\\begin{align*}1&=2a\\\\a&=\\frac12\\\\1&=-2b\\\\b&=-\\frac12.\\end{align*}$$\nTherefore,\n$$\\frac{1}{(n-1)(n+1)}=\\frac12\\left(\\frac{1}{n-1}-\\frac{1}{n+1}\\right).$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 48
      },
      {
        "id": "p32-q6b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Hence, simplify the sum\n$$\\frac{1}{2\\cdot4}+\\frac{1}{4\\cdot6}+\\cdots+\\frac{1}{n\\cdot(n+2)}.$$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\displaystyle\\frac{n}{4(n+2)}$.\n\nUse partial fractions; the intermediate reciprocal terms cancel.",
          "worked": "The denominators increase by $2$, so $n$ is even. From part (a),\n$$\\frac{1}{k(k+2)}=\\frac12\\left(\\frac1k-\\frac{1}{k+2}\\right).$$\nLet $S_n$ denote the required sum. Then\n$$\\begin{align*}S_n&=\\frac12\\left[\\left(\\frac12-\\frac14\\right)+\\left(\\frac14-\\frac16\\right)+\\cdots+\\left(\\frac1n-\\frac{1}{n+2}\\right)\\right]\\\\S_n&=\\frac12\\left(\\frac12-\\frac{1}{n+2}\\right)\\\\S_n&=\\frac{n}{4(n+2)}.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 42
      },
      {
        "id": "p32-q6c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Hence, evaluate\n$$\\frac{1}{2\\cdot4}+\\frac{1}{4\\cdot6}+\\cdots+\\frac{1}{98\\cdot100}.$$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\displaystyle\\frac{49}{200}$.",
          "worked": "Substitute $n=98$ into part (b):\n$$\\begin{align*}S_{98}&=\\frac{98}{4(98+2)}\\\\S_{98}&=\\frac{98}{400}\\\\S_{98}&=\\frac{49}{200}.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 18
      }
    ]
  },
